<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of the Covenant</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of the Covenant is the name scholars give to the collection of laws in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a> (chapters 20 to 23) in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>. Its provisions on injury, property and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> closely parallel earlier Mesopotamian law codes, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a>. It is cited as a channel through which the legal legacy of the Babylonian Empire reached later Western legal thought.</p></div>
